      "id": "verbosity_explain_water",
      "category": "verbosity_fingerprint",
      "check_type": "length_range",
      "passed": false,
      "errored": false,
      "error": null,
      "latency_ms": 2705.8,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 715,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical compound made up of two elements: **hydrogen and oxygen**. Its chemical formula is **$H_2O$**—meaning every single molecule of water consists of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom.\n\nHowever, water is much more than a simple chemical formula. It is the defining substance of our planet and the fundamental prerequisite for life as we know it. \n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is, looking at its properties, its role on Earth, and its importance to life:\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Unique States of Water\nWater is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three physical states:\n*   **Liquid:** The fluid we drink, swim in, and use to irrigate crops.\n*   **Solid (Ice):** Frozen water found in glaciers, snow, and ice cubes. (Uniquely, solid water is *less dense* than liquid water, which is why ice floats).\n*   **Gas (Vapor/Steam):** Invisible water vapor in the air that forms clouds, humidity, and fog.\n\n### 2. Remarkable Chemical Properties\nWater has several bizarre and unique scientific properties that make it special:\n*   **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Because of its chemical structure, water can dissolve more substances (like salt, sugar, and minerals) than any other liquid. This is how our blood transports nutrients throughout our bodies.\n*   **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb and release large amounts of heat without drastically changing its own temperature. This helps regulate the climate of the entire planet and keeps human bodies from overheating.\n*   **Cohesion and Adhesion:** Water molecules like to stick to each other (cohesion—which creates surface tension, allowing insects to walk on water) and to other surfaces (adhesion—which helps water travel up the roots of giant trees).\n\n### 3. Water on Earth\n*   **Planetary Coverage:** About **71% of the Earth’s surface** is covered in water, mostly by the oceans.\n*   **Scarcity of Fresh Water:** Despite Earth being the \"Blue Planet,\" about **96.5% of all Earth's water is saltwater** in the oceans. Only about **2.5% is freshwater**, and most of that is locked away in glaciers, ice caps, or buried deep underground. Less than 1% of Earth's water is easily accessible for human use.\n\n### 4. Water and Life\nWater is the cradle of life. The first living organisms originated in the oceans billions of years ago, and modern biology is entirely dependent on it:\n*   **Inside Us:** The human body is made up of roughly **60% water**. The brain is about 73% water, the lungs are about 83%, and even bones are about 31% water. \n*   **Biological Functions:** Water flushes toxins out of waste, cushions joints, regulates body temperature, and acts as the medium for all metabolic and cellular processes. Without water, human cells would quickly shrivel and die; a person can only survive a few days without it.\n\n### Summary\nIn short, water is a simple molecule with extraordinary properties. It sculpts the landscape of our planet, drives our weather and climate, and flows through the veins of every living thing, making it arguably the most precious resource in the universe."
